# Workspace line-ending policy. Canonical template -- copied verbatim into every
# workspace-managed repository. Do not edit a copy in place; edit this file.
#
# Why this exists at all: with core.autocrlf=true (the common Windows default),
# git rewrites LF blobs to CRLF at checkout for any path with no eol attribute.
# The repository stays LF while the working tree drifts, and line-ending-sensitive
# tools -- gofmt, cargo fmt, shell shebangs, byte-exact generated-file checks --
# then fail on content that is correct in git. `git archive` compounds it by
# honouring autocrlf too, so even an export can disagree with the repository.
#
# Two rules that are easy to get backwards:
#   * `git add --renormalize .` stages nothing when the blobs are already LF.
#     The repair for a stale checkout is a re-checkout, not a renormalise.
#   * Re-checking out a repository that has no eol attribute makes it WORSE,
#     because autocrlf performs the conversion. This file must be committed
#     first, then the working tree refreshed.
#
# Verify with `git ls-files --eol`, which reports index and working-tree
# encoding separately. Never infer line endings from `git archive` output.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows tooling does not reliably tolerate LF in these formats.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.sln text eol=crlf
